description:
FILED s536d (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-08), the residue #4625 / #3493's fix left on purpose (scratch/s536d/pr/b6.pl).  PRE-EXISTING (base f5f95d14 gives the same answer).

REPRODUCER (probed vs perl 5.40.3):

    sub f { my @env = ("A=1", "B=2", "A=3"); { chomp(my @env = grep { s/^A=// } @env); print "1 @env\n"; } print "2 ", scalar(@env), "\n"; }
    f();
    sub h { my $x = 5; { foo(my $x = $x + 1); print "4 $x\n" } print "5 $x\n" } sub foo { $_[0] } h();

perl: `1 1 3` / `2 3` / `4 6` / `5 5`.   PCL: `1 1 3` / `2 2` / `4 6` / `5 6` -- the outer variable was overwritten.

WHY: a `my` is visible only AFTER its statement, so the statement's other mentions of the name read the OUTER variable.  A fresh `let` around the statement would shadow them, so both embedded-`my` arms (Pl/Parser2.pm `_blk_expr` and #3431's `_decl_init_embedded_my_syms`) keep the old path when `_stmt_mentions_again` (s536d: ONE predicate for both): the statement writes the outer variable / the shared cell.  perl/t/op/magic.t:86 has the shape (`chomp (my @env = grep { s/^$key=// } @env)`), harmless there.
FIX DIRECTION: the self-referential-init idiom the scalar declaration arm already has (`p-box-init`: the init is evaluated in the OUTER environment, inside the let binding): lower the statement's other mentions against the outer binding -- e.g. bind the fresh container under a temporary, evaluate the statement with the declarator spelled as the temporary, then rebind the name for the remainder.  The binder can tell which mentions are the outer (`refers_to_decl`).  Bar: the reproducer = perl; magic.t's emission moves (explained); the sweep (declaration family).
